# Canonical identities for git log / shortlog / blame.
#
# This comment used to say "no commit in this repo uses jim.galasyn@confluent.io." That
# was false the moment it landed: the commit that ADDED this file — `235f509`, the
# squash-merge of PR #11, 2026-07-27 — is itself authored jim.galasyn@confluent.io. It is
# 1 of 18 commits as of 2026-08-01, and the only one.
#
# How it got there, since nothing local was committed with the work identity: GitHub
# squash-merge re-attributed it to the account's PUBLIC email, which is the work address,
# instead of to the branch commit's author. Confirmed 2026-08-01 against the PR itself —
# #11's two branch commits (`50fb8fa`, `c117752`) are both authored
# jim.galasyn@hotmail.com, and the squashed result is not. exoclimate #1 shows the same
# thing.
#
# It is INTERMITTENT, which is why it went unnoticed: five same-era squash merges
# preserved the branch author, two of them merged within seconds of a re-attributed one
# (jax-solitons #74 at 14:06:36 kept hotmail; this repo's #11 at 14:06:41 did not). The
# trigger is undetermined, and two explanations are now ruled out (checked 2026-08-01
# with the `user:email` scope): `gh api user/emails` reports jim.galasyn@confluent.io
# still PRIMARY, verified and public — the state it was in when this fired — and all
# seven 07-27-era squash commits, re-attributed and preserved alike, carry committer
# `GitHub <noreply@github.com>` with a valid signature, so the merge path does not
# distinguish them either.
# So the confluent.io mapping below is CORRECTIVE, not defensive: it rewrites a real
# commit, and the mechanism that produced it can fire again on any future PR.
#
# Mapped rather than rewritten: `235f509` is pushed, this repo is PUBLIC and publishes to
# PyPI as systemd-proc, and rewriting would change every SHA from it forward to remove an
# address that stays recoverable from the public event history regardless.
#
# The author name also appears as both "Jim" (16 commits) and "Jim Galasyn" (2); both map
# to one canonical form. `shortlog` is user-facing for a published package.
#
# Counts are a snapshot including this commit; recompute after the fact with
#   git log --format='%an <%ae>' | sort | uniq -c
#
# Format: Proper Name <proper@email> <commit@email>

Jim Galasyn <jim.galasyn@hotmail.com> <jim.galasyn@hotmail.com>
Jim Galasyn <jim.galasyn@hotmail.com> <jim.galasyn@confluent.io>
